WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.232
Before writing nested queries, inspect the underlying rows.

00:00:04.232 --> 00:00:08.612
Run this simple SELECT and look at the patient IDs and ages.

00:00:08.612 --> 00:00:13.397
Later, when the subquery returns a set of IDs or an average age,

00:00:13.397 --> 00:00:17.817
you will be able to connect the result back to real rows in the teaching dataset.

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:20.572
This is also a good real-world habit.

00:00:20.572 --> 00:00:24.817
Understand the source tables before debugging a complex query.

00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:30.707
Connect this idea to a hospital identifying patients above a benchmark or with no follow-up.

00:00:30.707 --> 00:00:36.387
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:36.387 --> 00:00:44.352
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:44.352 --> 00:00:50.102
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:50.102 --> 00:00:55.886
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
